<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rashid Ali al-Gaylani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1892">1892</a>–<a href="/years/1965">1965</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rashid Ali al-Gaylani was an Iraqi nationalist politician who served several times as prime minister. In April 1941 he took power in a coup backed by four army officers known as the Golden Square and sought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> support against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. Defeated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-iraqi-war">Anglo-Iraqi War</a> of May 1941, he fled and spent the rest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rashid-ali-al-gaylani.jpg" alt="Rashid Ali al-Gaylani" width="250" height="370" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rashid_Ali_al-Gaylani.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rashid Ali was the son of Sayyid Abd al-Wahhab al-Gailani and belonged to a prominent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> family whose name points to an origin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and which traces its ancestry to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a>; he also descended from Circassian governors of the Ottoman period. He studied at the law school in Baghdad and practised as a lawyer before entering politics.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1924 Yasin al-Hashimi made him minister of justice in his first government, and he was elected president of the Chamber of Deputies in 1925 and 1926. Both men were nationalists opposed to any British part in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>'s internal politics. They rejected the Anglo-Iraqi Treaty signed by the government of Nuri as-Said in 1930 and formed the Party of National Brotherhood. Gaylani was prime minister for the first time from March to November 1933.</p><p>He returned to the premiership on 31 March 1940, after the early death of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazi">Ghazi</a>, when Emir Abdul-Illah was regent for the four-year-old Faisal II. Gaylani refused Allied troops passage through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, rejected calls to break with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascist-italy">Fascist Italy</a> and sent his justice minister, Naji Shawkat, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a>, where on 5 July 1940 he met the German ambassador, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-von-papen">Franz von Papen</a>. Britain imposed economic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a>, and under pressure from the regent he resigned on 31 January 1941.</p><p>With the Golden Square he then planned to assassinate the regent, who discovered the plot on 31 March and fled. The coup followed on 1 April, and on 3 April a National Defence Government took office under Gaylani. He sent artillery against the RAF base at Habbaniya. On 2 May the base's commander struck first at the Iraqi positions; within a week the Iraqis left the escarpment, and British forces advanced through Fallujah on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. Gaylani fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> on 30 May. An armistice was signed the next day, and the regent returned on 1 June. Immediately afterwards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farhud">Farhud</a>, a violent pogrom against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, took place in Baghdad.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After British and Soviet forces invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> on 25 August 1941 he went on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> received him and he was recognised as leader of an Iraqi government in exile. On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s defeat he found refuge in Saudi Arabia. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> only after the revolution of 1958 that overthrew the monarchy, plotted a revolt against the government of Abdul Karim Kassem and was sentenced to death. Pardoned in July 1961, he lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, where he died on 28 August 1965 at the age of 72.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rashid Ali al-Gaylani” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The period saw the Simele massacre of Assyrians in 1933, the Arab world's first military coup in 1936, and in 1941 <b>Rashid Ali</b>'s pro-Axis coup, the Anglo-Iraqi War and the Farhud pogrom against Baghdad's Jews.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-iraq">Kingdom of Iraq</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. Died in Beirut, in what is now <a href="/countries/lebanon">Lebanon</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.32,44.37&amp;year=1939" aria-label="Baghdad on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5684 954 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Rashid Ali al-Gaylani"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6134 1224h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6134 1224h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.32,44.37&amp;year=1939">What was here in 1939? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
